Enhancement: Preserve sparse regions and add restore concurrency and cleanup options

Sparse restores (`--sparse`) now punch holes for every run of zero bytes,
including zeros in the middle or at the end of a blob. Restoring a sparse
snapshot over an existing, fully allocated file now deallocates the zero
regions instead of leaving stale data behind or filling the target disk. On
filesystems without hole-punching support, zeros are written explicitly so the
restored content stays correct.

`restore` also gains:

* `--file-workers` to limit the number of files restored concurrently. Bandwidth
  can be limited independently with the global `--limit-download` option.
* `--cleanup-on-cancel` (enabled by default), which removes files created by an
  interrupted restore while keeping files that pre-existed at the target.
* a final summary listing every item that failed to restore together with its
  error. Individual item failures no longer abort the whole restore when the
  error callback continues.

